                                <h2 id=3D"introduction">Introduction</h2><p=
>Building advanced AI agents has been greatly simplified by high-level fram=
eworks like LangChain and CrewAI.</p><p>However, these frameworks often hid=
e the underlying logic that makes an AI agent tick.</p><p>More specifically=
, under the hood, many such frameworks use the ReAct (Reasoning and Acting)=
 pattern to let large language models (LLMs) think through problems and use=
 tools to act on the world=E2=80=8B.</p><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-c=
ard"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8=
078bd13e748/content/images/2026/01/https-3a-2f-2fsubstack-post-media-s3-ama=
zonaws-com-2fpublic-2fimages-2fd02b2eaa-16c3-4f92-8f97-06329fbcccd4_716x550=
-6.gif" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"eager" width=3D"450" height=
=3D"345" fetchpriority=3D"high"></figure><p>For example, an agent in CrewAI=
 typically alternates between reasoning about a task and acting (using a to=
ol) to gather information or execute steps, following the ReAct paradigm=E2=
=80=8B.</p><p>This approach enhances an LLM agent=E2=80=99s ability to hand=
le complex tasks and decisions by combining chain-of-thought reasoning with=
 external tool use=E2=80=8B.</p><p>While frameworks like CrewAI implement t=
his logic for you and you should also keep using them in production systems=
 (also because they are built by experts), often times, relying on pre-pack=
aged libraries without understanding their inner workings can lead to confu=
sion=E2=80=8B.</p><p>You might use an agent framework and wonder how it dec=
ides to, say, perform a web search or a calculation as part of answering a =
question.</p><p>An example is shown below where the Agent is going through =
a series of thought activities before producing a response.</p><figure clas=
s=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3f=
df6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/image-28.png" cla=
ss=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" height=3D"1594"></=
figure><p>In this article, we=E2=80=99ll demystify that process by building=
 a ReAct agent from scratch using only Python and an LLM.</p><figure class=
=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fd=
f6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/image-29.png" clas=
s=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" height=3D"710"></fi=
gure><p>By doing so, we gain full control over the agent=E2=80=99s behavior=
, making it easier to optimize and troubleshoot=E2=80=8B.</p><p>We=E2=80=99=
ll use OpenAI, but if you prefer to do it with Ollama locally, an open-sour=
ce tool for running LLMs locally, with a model like Llama3 to power the age=
nt, you can do that as well.</p><p>Along the way, we=E2=80=99ll explain the=
 ReAct pattern, design an agent loop that interleaves reasoning and tool us=
age, and implement multiple tools that the agent can call.</p><p>The goal i=
s to help you understand both the theory and implementation of ReAct agents=
.</p><p>By the end, you=E2=80=99ll have a working agent and a clear picture=
 of how frameworks like CrewAI leverage ReAct internally.</p><p>Let's begin=
!</p><hr><h2 id=3D"what-is-react-pattern">What is ReAct pattern?</h2><p>ReA=
ct (short for Reasoning and Acting) is a paradigm for AI agent design where=
 an agent uses chain-of-thought reasoning and tool-using actions in aggrega=
tion.</p><p>Instead of generating a direct answer in one step, a ReAct agen=
t thinks step-by-step and can perform intermediate actions (like looking so=
mething up or calculating a value) before finalizing its answer.</p><figure=
 class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/=
df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/image-30.png=
" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" height=3D"756=
"></figure><p>To get a clear perspective on this, Like think about how ReAc=
t pattern works.</p><h3 id=3D"sample-1">Sample 1</h3><p>Formally, an LLM fo=
llowing ReAct generates reasoning traces (the =E2=80=9CThoughts=E2=80=9D) a=
nd task-specific actions (calls to tools) in an interleaved manner=E2=80=8B=
. This means the model=E2=80=99s output might look something like:</p><ul><=
li>Thought: I should calculate the total.</li><li>Action: <code>Calculator(=
"123 + 456")</code></li><li>Observation: <code>579</code></li><li>Thought: =
Now I have the sum; next, I need to multiply it.</li><li>Action: <code>Calc=
ulator("579 * 789")</code></li><li>Observation: <code>456,831</code>.</li><=
li>Thought: I have the final result.</li><li>Final Answer: <code>456,831</c=
ode>.</li></ul><p>The reasoning traces (the chain of thoughts) help the mod=
el plan and keep track of what to do next, while the actions let it consult=
 external sources or perform calculations to gather information it otherwis=
e wouldn=E2=80=99t have=E2=80=8B inherent access to.</p><p>In effect, the m=
odel isn=E2=80=99t limited to its internal knowledge; it can reach out to t=
ools, databases, or the internet as needed and then reason about the result=
s.</p><p>This significantly enhances what the agent can do.</p><p>IBM descr=
ibes ReAct agents as ones that use an LLM =E2=80=9Cbrain=E2=80=9D to coordi=
nate reasoning and action, enabling interactions with the environment in a =
structured but adaptable way=E2=80=8B.</p><p>Unlike a basic chatbot that an=
swers with whatever is in its static knowledge, a ReAct agent can think, se=
arch, calculate, and then combine the results into an answer.</p><h3 id=3D"=
sample-2">Sample 2</h3><p>Consider the output of an Agent I built in a mult=
i-agent system (we'll get to the code shortly):</p><figure class=3D"kg-card=
 kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-=
4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/Screenshot-2025-04-15-at-7.07=
.56-PM.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"1856" hei=
ght=3D"345"></figure><p>In the example shown above, we see a live trace of =
an AI News Reporter Agent executing a task using the ReAct paradigm. The ag=
ent has been asked to create a news headline related to the "Agent2Agent Pr=
otocol". However, rather than jumping to conclusions, it reasons step by st=
ep, as seen in its structured trace.</p><p>Let=E2=80=99s break this down:</=
p><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost=
.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/Scr=
eenshot-2025-04-15-at-7.07.56-PM.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=
=3D"lazy" width=3D"1856" height=3D"345"></figure><ul><li>Agent Role: <code>=
News Reporter</code> =E2=80=93 This agent specializes in curating and valid=
ating news content.</li><li>Task: The agent has been instructed to generate=
 a news headline and ensure it's relevant to the Agent2Agent Protocol.</li>=
<li>Thought: The agent first reasons internally that it should validate the=
 information by performing a quick search for any recent updates about the =
protocol. This is the reasoning part of the ReAct cycle.</li><li>Action: It=
 proceeds to use a tool labeled <code>Search the internet</code>, passing i=
n a structured JSON input with the query: <code>"Agent2Agent Protocol news =
November 2023"</code>. This is the acting step where the agent leverages ex=
ternal tools to gather real-world data.</li></ul><figure class=3D"kg-card k=
g-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b=
12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/Screenshot-2025-04-15-at-7.13.2=
6-PM.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"1856" heigh=
t=3D"713"></figure><ul><li>Tool Output: It contains the results retrieved b=
y the search tool=E2=80=94potentially news snippets, article summaries, or =
relevant URLs.</li></ul><p>This illustrates the power of combining structur=
ed thought and external actions: rather than relying on the model's interna=
l knowledge alone, the agent cross-checks facts using tools.</p><p>It's an =
example of how the ReAct pattern encourages transparency, accuracy, and ver=
ifiability in agent behavior=E2=80=94an essential feature for any system ta=
sked with real-world information synthesis. </p><p>You can imagine scaling =
this further with multi-agent setups: a News Collector gathers raw feeds, a=
 Fact Verifier checks reliability, and this News Reporter constructs the he=
adline=E2=80=94all coordinating asynchronously using reasoning and tool-bas=
ed actions.</p><p>Here's the implementation if you want to replicate the sa=
me output above.</p><div class=3D"kg-card kg-callout-card kg-callout-card-b=
lue"><div class=3D"kg-callout-emoji">=F0=9F=92=A1</div><div class=3D"kg-cal=
lout-text">Make sure you have created a <code spellcheck=3D"false" style=3D=
"white-space: pre-wrap;">.env</code> file with the <code spellcheck=3D"fals=
e" style=3D"white-space: pre-wrap;">OPENAI_API_KEY</code> specified in it. =
It will make things much easier and faster for you. Also, add these two lin=
es of code to handle asynchronous operations within a Jupyter Notebook envi=
ronment, which will allow us to make asynchronous calls smoothly to your Cr=
ew Agent.</div></div>
