agent "thinks" in natural language, plans its next move, and uses external =
tools to carry out actions.</p><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img=
 src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e7=
48/content/images/2025/04/image-31.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=
=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" height=3D"1594"></figure><p>But that=E2=80=99s not=
 all. Look at one more thing towards the end of this verbose output:</p><fi=
gure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c=
/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/Screensh=
ot-2025-04-15-at-7.34.59-PM.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"laz=
y" width=3D"1126" height=3D"457"></figure><p>Under the hood, this ReAct-sty=
le behavior is governed by a very specific format template=E2=80=94what we =
call the action protocol. When using tools like <code>SerperDevTool</code>,=
 CrewAI instructs the LLM to follow a rigid response schema. This helps ens=
ure agents interact with tools in a safe, deterministic, and interpretable =
manner.</p><p>First, we have the Agent tool prompt format:</p><div class=3D=
"code-toolbar"><pre class=3D"language-python" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D=
"language-python"><span class=3D"token triple-quoted-string string">"""
You ONLY have access to the following tools,
and should NEVER make up tools that are not listed here:

Tool Name: Search the internet
Tool Arguments: {'search_query': {'description':
                 'Mandatory search query you want to use
                 to search the internet', 'type': 'str'}}
Tool Description: A tool that can be used to
                  search the internet with a search_query.

"""</span></code></pre><div class=3D"toolbar"><div class=3D"toolbar-item"><=
button class=3D"copy-to-clipboard-button" type=3D"button" data-copy-state=
=3D"copy"><span>Copy</span></button></div></div></div><p>This is part of th=
e tool context injected into the LLM prompt. It tells the agent:</p><ul><li=
>What tools are available.</li><li>What arguments are required.</li><li>Tha=
t it must not invent tools or go off-protocol.</li></ul><p>This creates str=
ong constraints around agent behavior=E2=80=94which is important when you w=
ant to avoid hallucinations or misuse of capabilities.</p><p>The prompt als=
o includes this critical instruction:</p><div class=3D"code-toolbar"><pre c=
lass=3D"language-python" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-python"><sp=
an class=3D"token triple-quoted-string string">"""
IMPORTANT: Use the following format in your response:

```
Thought: you should always think about what to do
Action: the action to take, only one name of
[Search the internet], just the name, as it's written.

Action Input: the input to the action, just a
simple JSON object, enclosed in curly braces,
using " to wrap keys and values.

Observation: the result of the action
```
"""</span></code></pre><div class=3D"toolbar"><div class=3D"toolbar-item"><=
button class=3D"copy-to-clipboard-button" type=3D"button" data-copy-state=
=3D"copy"><span>Copy</span></button></div></div></div><p>This is the <em>re=
asoning + acting</em> loop spelled out in literal terms:</p><ol><li>Thought=
: The agent expresses its internal reasoning.</li><li>Action: The agent pic=
ks the tool to use=E2=80=94verbatim.</li><li>Action Input: Arguments for th=
e tool, formatted as strict JSON.</li><li>Observation: What the tool return=
ed (i.e., the raw output).</li></ol><p>This schema ensures that tools can b=
e executed reliably, logged consistently, and traced clearly. And crucially=
=E2=80=94it=E2=80=99s LLM-friendly. Everything is written in natural langua=
ge that the model understands and responds to.</p><p>Once all information i=
s gathered, the agent is told to conclude like this:</p><div class=3D"code-=
toolbar"><pre class=3D"language-python" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"langu=
age-python"><span class=3D"token triple-quoted-string string">"""
Once all necessary information is gathered,
return the following format:

```
Thought: I now know the final answer
Final Answer: the final answer to
the original input question
```
"""</span></code></pre><div class=3D"toolbar"><div class=3D"toolbar-item"><=
button class=3D"copy-to-clipboard-button" type=3D"button" data-copy-state=
=3D"copy"><span>Copy</span></button></div></div></div><p>This signals the e=
nd of the reasoning chain. At this point, the agent has completed its resea=
rch and can produce a confident, top-level answer.</p><p>That=E2=80=99s the=
 essence of ReAct, which is implemented seamlessly using CrewAI.</p><h3 id=
=3D"but-why-does-this-matter">But why does this matter?</h3><p>ReAct was in=
troduced as a major step in making LLM-based agents more reliable and power=
ful=E2=80=8B.</p><p>By having the model explain its thought process and che=
ck facts via tools, we reduce problems like hallucination and error propaga=
tion.</p><p>The original ReAct research by Yao et al. showed that this appr=
oach can overcome hallucination in question answering by letting the model =
retrieve real information (e.g. querying Wikipedia) to verify its facts=E2=
=80=8B.</p><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://stor=
age.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/20=
25/04/Screenshot-2025-04-15-at-6.13.51-PM.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" =
loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"1271" height=3D"765"></figure><p>It also improves=
 the transparency of the agent=E2=80=99s decision-making, as we can inspect=
 the chain of thought for debugging or trustworthiness=E2=80=8B.</p><p>Over=
all, the ReAct pattern turns a passive LLM into an active problem solver th=
at can break down complex tasks and interact with external data sources, mu=
ch like an autonomous assistant.</p><p>This also explains why it is widely =
used in almost Agentic frameworks. The actual implementation could vary but=
 everything does connect back to something that's derived from a ReAct patt=
ern.</p><p>To reiterate, this format:</p><ul><li>Forces your LLM to operate=
 step by step,</li><li>Clearly separates thinking from acting,</li><li>Guar=
antees deterministic input-output behavior for tools,</li><li>And produces =
traceable reasoning chains you can inspect or debug.</li></ul><h3 id=3D"rea=
soning-acting-how-react-agents-work">Reasoning + Acting: How ReAct Agents W=
ork</h3><p>A ReAct agent operates in a loop of Thought =E2=86=92 Action =E2=
=86=92 Observation, repeating until it reaches a solution or a final answer=
.</p><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.gh=
ost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/=
image-33.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" h=
eight=3D"645"></figure><p>This is analogous to how humans solve problems:</=
p><ul><li>we think about what to do</li><li>perform an action (like looking=
 something up or doing a calculation),</li><li>observe the result</li><li>a=
nd then incorporate that into our next thought.</li></ul><p>The ReAct frame=
work uses prompt engineering to enforce this structured approach, alternati=
ng the model=E2=80=99s thoughts and actions/observations=E2=80=8B.</p><p>He=
re=E2=80=99s a step-by-step breakdown of the ReAct cycle in an AI agent:</p=
><ul><li>Thought: The Agent (powered by an LLM) analyzes the user=E2=80=99s=
 query and internal context, and produces a reasoning step in natural langu=
age. This is typically not shown to the end user but is part of the agent=
=E2=80=99s self-talk. For example: =E2=80=9CThe question asks for the popul=
ation of a country; I should use a web search to find the latest figure.=E2=
=80=9D</li></ul><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https:/=
/storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/imag=
es/2025/04/image-34.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=
=3D"2000" height=3D"622"></figure><ul><li>Action: Based on the thought, the=
 agent decides on an external tool or operation to perform. It outputs a pr=
escribed format indicating the action. For instance: <code>Action: WebSearc=
h("population of Canada 2023")</code>. The agent essentially =E2=80=9Ccalls=
=E2=80=9D a function (tool) by name, often with some input parameters.</li>=
</ul><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.gh=
ost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/=
image-42.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" h=
eight=3D"1297"></figure><ul><li>Observation: The agent=E2=80=99s environmen=
t (our code) executes the requested action and returns the result (observat=
ion) back to the agent. For example, the web search tool might return: =E2=
=80=9C<code>Observation: The population of Canada in 2023 is 38 million.</c=
ode>=E2=80=9D This observation is fed into the agent=E2=80=99s context.</li=
></ul><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.g=
host.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04=
/image-41.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" =
height=3D"1372"></figure><ul><li>The agent takes the new information and go=
es back to step 1 (another Thought). It will reason with the fresh data. In=
 our example, it might think: =E2=80=9CNow I have the population figure; I =
can answer the question.=E2=80=9D</li></ul><figure class=3D"kg-card kg-imag=
e-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed2-17ac-4b12-a69=
3-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/image-40.png" class=3D"kg-image" alt=
=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"2000" height=3D"1299"></figure><ol><li>This=
 Thought/Action/Observation cycle repeats, allowing the agent to chain mult=
iple tool uses if needed (search, then maybe a calculation, then another se=
arch, etc.). Eventually, the agent decides it can answer the user. At that =
point, instead of an Action, it outputs a Final Answer (sometimes marked as=
 <code>Answer:</code> or <code>Final Answer:</code> in the format).</li></o=
l><p>As we shall see shortly in the implementation from scratch, throughout=
 this process, the agent maintains the conversation and its own intermediat=
e steps.</p><p>Each Thought and Observation can be appended to the dialogue=
 context so the LLM remembers what it has done so far.</p><figure class=3D"=
kg-card kg-image-card"><img src=3D"https://storage.ghost.io/c/3f/df/3fdf6ed=
2-17ac-4b12-a693-8078bd13e748/content/images/2025/04/image-43.png" class=3D=
"kg-image" alt=3D"" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"1688" height=3D"1083"></figur=
e><p>This is crucial for coherence. The end result is that the agent effect=
ively plans its approach on the fly, mixing reasoning and acting.</p><p>Thi=
s dynamic approach is much more adaptable than a rigid script or a single-t=
urn response. It allows handling unforeseen sub-tasks, similar to how human=
s adjust plans when new information comes up=E2=80=8B.</p><p>It=E2=80=99s i=
mportant to note that all these =E2=80=9CThought=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9CActi=
on=E2=80=9D annotations are not magical features of the LLM=E2=80=94they co=
me from how we prompt the model.</p><p>As we shall see below, we explicitly=
 instruct the model to format its responses in this structured way. In othe=
r words, ReAct is implemented via carefully crafted prompt templates and pa=
rsing logic, not via any built-in LLM ability.</p><p>The LLM is guided to b=
ehave like an agent that reasons and acts, through the examples and instruc=
tions we give it.</p><p>Now that we understand the ReAct pattern conceptual=
ly, we can start building our own agent that follows this logic. We=E2=80=
=99ll need a language model to serve as the agent=E2=80=99s brain, some too=
ls the agent can use, and a loop that ties them together.</p><p>In the next=
 section, we=E2=80=99ll step away from CrewAI and build a ReAct agent from =
scratch=E2=80=94in pure Python, using only local LLMs and a simple set of t=
ool definitions. You'll see that everything we covered here is not magic=E2=
=80=94it=E2=80=99s just smart prompt design combined with controlled I/O.</=
p><div class=3D"kg-card kg-callout-card kg-callout-card-blue"><div class=3D=
"kg-callout-emoji">=F0=9F=92=A1</div><div class=3D"kg-callout-text">Make su=
re you have created a <code spellcheck=3D"false" style=3D"white-space: pre-=
wrap;">.env</code> file with the <code spellcheck=3D"false" style=3D"white-=
space: pre-wrap;">OPENAI_API_KEY</code> specified in it. It will make thing=
s much easier and faster for you. Also, add these two lines of code to hand=
le asynchronous operations within a Jupyter Notebook environment, which wil=
l allow us to make asynchronous calls smoothly to your Crew Agent.</div></d=
iv><h2 id=3D"react-implementation-from-scratch">ReAct Implementation from S=
cratch</h2><p>Below, we shall implement a ReAct Agent in two ways:</p><ul><=
li>Manually executing each step for better clarity.</li><li>Without manual =
intervention to fully automate the Reasoning and Action process.</li></ul><=
p>You can download the code below:</p>

